%I A398233 #5 Aug 11 2026 00:43:23
%S A398233 0,0,0,0,1,6,8,24,43,74,148,245,441,753,1178,1742,2605,3805,5589,8256,
%T A398233 11909,17073,24364,34257,46272,65358,89272,121195,164661,225420,
%U A398233 296552,392092,510300,671583,881818,1157090,1487233,1936524,2492860,3193102,4072445,5212816
%N A398233 a(n) is the total collision excess among unordered pairwise sums with repetition of the distinct multinomial coefficient values of order n.
%C A398233 Let B(n) be the set of distinct numbers n!/(p_1!*...*p_r!), where (p_1, ..., p_r) ranges over the partitions of n.
%C A398233 Since the number r of parts is unrestricted, B(n) contains all distinct multinomial coefficient values of order n, independently of the number of variables.
%C A398233 Thus a(n) is the total collision excess among the unordered pairwise sums with repetition of elements of B(n). A sum having r representations contributes r - 1 to a(n).
%C A398233 For n >= 3, a(n) is the common value of the last three entries in row n of A398232.
%C A398233 Appending a part 1 to every partition of n shows that (n + 1)*B(n) is a subset of B(n + 1). Since scaling preserves pair-sum representations and adjoining elements cannot decrease the collision excess, a(n + 1) >= a(n).
%C A398233 In fact, a(n + 1) > a(n) for n >= 5. For n >= 16, let u = (n + 1)!/(12*(n - 4)!), v = (n + 1)!/(4*(n - 4)!), and w = (n + 1)!/(6*(n - 4)!). Then v and w belong to the embedded copy (n + 1)*B(n), whereas u does not, and u + v = 2*w adds a new representation. The cases 5 <= n <= 15 are verified directly. See the Huber link for a proof.
%C A398233 A398238(n) counts the distinct pairwise sums having more than one representation, whereas a(n) counts their total collision excess. Hence a(n) >= A398238(n), with equality if and only if no pairwise sum has more than two representations.
%C A398233 The first sum having three representations occurs at n = 9: 84 + 1680 = 252 + 1512 = 504 + 1260. Embedding this identity into every higher order shows that a(n) > A398238(n) for n >= 9.
%C A398233 A398265 refines a(n) by recording, for each k, the number of pairwise sums having at least k representations. Consequently, for n >= 5, a(n) is the sum of row n of A398265.
%C A398233 For odd n >= 7, the trinomial collision family gives a(n) >= floor((n - 3)/4). For even n, append a part 1 to every indexing partition of the corresponding family for n - 1; this multiplies every coefficient and collision sum by n and preserves the number of distinct colliding sums. Hence the same bound holds for all n >= 7.
%C A398233 For q >= 3, let N(q) = Sum_{p prime, p <= q} p*floor(log(q)/log(p)). The set B(N(q)) contains an arithmetic progression of length q. Moreover, for n >= N(q) + q, the set B(n) contains n - N(q) - q + 1 scaled copies whose internal pair-sum intervals are disjoint. Hence a(n) >= (n - N(q) - q + 1)*(q - 1)*(q - 2)/2. See the Huber link for a proof.
%H A398233 Felix Huber, <a href="/A398233/a398233_1.txt">Arithmetic-progression lower bounds for A398233, A398238, and A398263</a>
%H A398233 Felix Huber, <a href="/A398233/a398233_2.txt">Strict monotonicity of A398233 and A398238</a>
%F A398233 a(n) = binomial(A070289(n) + 1, 2) - |{x + y : x, y in B(n), x <= y}|, where B(n) = {A210237(n, j) : 1 <= j <= A070289(n)}.
%F A398233 a(n) = A398238(n) + Sum_s max(r_n(s) - 2, 0), where r_n(s) = |{(x, y) : x, y in B(n), x <= y and x + y = s}|.
%F A398233 a(n) = A398232(n, n - 2) = A398232(n, n - 1) = A398232(n, n) for n >= 3.
%F A398233 a(n) = Sum_{k = 2 .. A398263(n)} A398265(n, k) for n >= 5.
%F A398233 a(n) >= A398230(n) for n >= 3.
%F A398233 a(n) >= A398238(n) + A398263(n) - 2.
%F A398233 a(n) > A398238(n) for n >= 9.
%F A398233 a(n) > 0 if and only if n >= 5.
%F A398233 a(n + 1) > a(n) for n >= 5.
%F A398233 a(n) >= floor((n - 3)/4) for n >= 7.
%F A398233 a(n) >= (q - 1)*(q - 2)/2 whenever n >= N(q) and q >= 3, where N(q) = Sum_{p prime, p <= q} p*floor(log(q)/log(p)).
%F A398233 a(n) >= (n - N(q) - q + 1)*(q - 1)*(q - 2)/2 whenever n >= N(q) + q and q >= 3.
%F A398233 liminf_{n -> infinity} a(n)/(n^2*log(n)) >= 1/8.
%e A398233 B(6) = {1, 6, 15, 20, 30, 60, 90, 120, 180, 360, 720}, so there are binomial(12, 2) = 66 unordered pairs but only 60 distinct pairwise sums; hence a(6) = 66 - 60 = 6.
%p A398233 A398233 := proc(n) option remember;
%p A398233     local b, i, j, l, m, s, t;
%p A398233     t := table();
%p A398233     b := proc(r, p, d)
%p A398233         local i;
%p A398233         if r = 0 then
%p A398233             t[n!/d] := 1;
%p A398233             return
%p A398233         end if;
%p A398233         for i from min(p, r) by -1 to 1 do
%p A398233             b(r - i, i, d*i!)
%p A398233         end do
%p A398233     end proc;
%p A398233     b(n, n, 1);
%p A398233     l := [indices(t, 'nolist')];
%p A398233     m := nops(l);
%p A398233     s := table();
%p A398233     for i to m do
%p A398233         for j from i to m do
%p A398233             s[l[i] + l[j]] := 1
%p A398233         end do
%p A398233     end do;
%p A398233     binomial(m + 1, 2) - numelems(s)
%p A398233 end proc:
%p A398233 seq(A398233(n), n = 1 .. 30);
%Y A398233 Cf. A070289, A210237, A398230, A398231, A398232, A398237, A398238, A398263, A398265, A398429.
%K A398233 nonn
%O A398233 1,6
%A A398233 _Felix Huber_, Aug 03 2026
